      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dane County was named after Nathan Dane, a Massachusetts delegate who helped carve Wisconsin out of the Northwest Territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dane_County%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Dane County was formed as a territorial county in 1836 and officially organized in 1839.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dane_County%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Settlers from New England arrived to settle Dane County during the 1840s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dane_County%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Madison serves as both the county seat of Dane County and the capital of Wisconsin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dane_County%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Dane County forms the core of the Madison metropolitan area and the broader Madison-Janesville-Beloit combined statistical area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dane_County%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
